00:00:09.116 --> 00:00:12.836
Thank you all. And thank you, Sid, for the introduction.

00:00:12.836 --> 00:00:20.396
So today we’re here to talk about how
we scale the AI, Agentic AI in production.

00:00:20.716 --> 00:00:25.876
And so here's how we'll spend the next 30 minutes together.

00:00:27.756 --> 00:00:35.516
So we'll first unpack what it takes to build reliable agents,
which is to do with the four pillars of the Trust Foundation.

00:00:35.916 --> 00:00:38.196
And then Miss Karen Chiah will take the stage

00:00:38.196 --> 00:00:45.756
and share how the Ministry of Education is harnessing AI
for operational excellence today.

00:00:46.116 --> 00:00:48.716
And this is a real story in production today.

00:00:48.716 --> 00:00:53.516
I will then explain how we built and scaled and what we learn.

00:00:53.516 --> 00:00:57.796
So you can take this back and scale in your own system.

00:00:57.996 --> 00:01:06.436
So let's begin. I hope you walked in here with a question
or a challenge in mind, and maybe it's one of these three, right?

00:01:06.436 --> 00:01:09.076
What problems can I solve with AI agents?

00:01:09.436 --> 00:01:11.156
How do I know if I can trust them?

00:01:12.356 --> 00:01:13.716
Or where do I even start?

00:01:15.076 --> 00:01:18.076
Whatever brought you here today, it comes back to one word.

00:01:19.036 --> 00:01:23.436
Just trust. We are all here today building something new.

00:01:24.396 --> 00:01:26.796
But here's the truth that doesn't change, right?

00:01:26.836 --> 00:01:30.516
The systems people rely on are the ones that they trust.

00:01:31.356 --> 00:01:35.756
And so how do we know that AI agents are worth that trust, right?

00:01:36.236 --> 00:01:38.836
And trust isn't really new for AWS.

00:01:39.036 --> 00:01:40.556
Let me show you what I mean.

00:01:42.316 --> 00:01:47.196
For the last 20 years, every leap
forward at AWS has been powered by one thing.

00:01:47.236 --> 00:01:55.116
Is your trust in us. With every chapter, we've worked to earn
it back by turning your trust into the invention that moves.

00:01:55.596 --> 00:02:02.756
So it could be from scaling with EC2 serverless
with Lambda SageMaker, AI and machine learning,

00:02:03.276 --> 00:02:06.596
and also Bedrock, giving you the choice of models.

00:02:07.716 --> 00:02:10.476
Which brings us back to the biggest leap yet, right?

00:02:11.596 --> 00:02:17.196
Just Agentic AI. Right. So we're moving
from an AI that answers to an AI that acts.

00:02:17.916 --> 00:02:21.316
At this point, it isn't about building smarter models.

00:02:21.316 --> 00:02:29.556
It's scaling AI agents into real
production where they run every day and every time.

00:02:30.116 --> 00:02:35.516
But here's the thing that scaling
AI agents isn't like scaling a model or a service,

00:02:36.076 --> 00:02:41.956
because building agents that you can actually
trust is pretty hard because an agent isn't really a model.

00:02:42.596 --> 00:02:50.596
It's actually a system, right? It reaches into your databases,
it reaches into your local files, your APIs, your sensors.

00:02:50.596 --> 00:02:57.436
It's basically impersonating you within a system and writing
back to the systems that runs your own business.

00:02:58.516 --> 00:03:01.676
Every one of these errors that you see is something.

00:03:01.756 --> 00:03:04.276
There are places where something can go wrong.

00:03:04.276 --> 00:03:06.956
If you have wrong data, you have wrong action.

00:03:07.116 --> 00:03:10.476
If you have a wrong permission, it's a wrong outcome.

00:03:10.476 --> 00:03:16.116
And when agent acts autonomously,
a small mistake doesn't really give a bad answer.

00:03:16.476 --> 00:03:18.996
It actually ends up taking a bad action.

00:03:18.996 --> 00:03:21.956
So the question is not that can we build agents?

00:03:21.956 --> 00:03:26.716
The question is more around how do you
build agents that you can run in production?

00:03:27.836 --> 00:03:30.076
Which comes back to the four pillars, right.

00:03:30.516 --> 00:03:33.316
And there's four questions that you need to ask yourself.

00:03:33.316 --> 00:03:37.996
Are they reliable? Is it transparent? Are they safe?

00:03:38.636 --> 00:03:44.236
And is it easy? It could be easy to build
or it could be easy for the consumer to consume.

00:03:44.236 --> 00:03:50.116
And getting these four things right,
trust becomes trust is no more a blocker.

00:03:50.116 --> 00:03:52.076
It actually becomes your advantage.

00:03:53.436 --> 00:03:57.396
And you might wonder what makes an agent that you can trust.

00:03:59.076 --> 00:04:01.076
It actually brings down to four layers,

00:04:02.236 --> 00:04:09.156
the first layer being a reliable and scalable cloud,
which allows you to provide the foundation that powers it all.

00:04:09.996 --> 00:04:13.676
The next layer would be your data, which is the most critical one,

00:04:13.676 --> 00:04:18.156
which should be consolidated and accessible
and not scattered across silos.

00:04:19.516 --> 00:04:28.236
Following by the integration, which is your APIs and connectors,
and that link your agent to the data and the different systems.

00:04:28.956 --> 00:04:33.716
Last but not least is a security compliance and access control.

00:04:33.996 --> 00:04:41.396
So your agents can operate safely and also
ensure that the data is being handled securely.

00:04:42.556 --> 00:04:47.516
Getting these four things right,
the agent goes into production getting them wrong.

00:04:47.516 --> 00:04:50.116
It actually ends up stuck in the pilot.

00:04:51.476 --> 00:04:54.516
So that's actually a lot to build. So we built it for you.

00:04:54.516 --> 00:05:00.000
This is Amazon Bedrock AgentCore. It's the foundation for building
agents that you can trust and scale at enterprise level.

